  {
   "slug": "what-that-warning-light-means",
   "title": "What that warning light on your dashboard means",
   "date": "2026-10-10T12:00:00",
   "excerpt": "Red means stop, amber means soon. A plain guide to the lights you are most likely to see.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Dashboard lights follow a simple traffic-light rule. Red means stop safely as soon as you can. Amber means something needs checking soon. Green and blue are just information.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:table --><figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Light</th><th>Colour</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Oil pressure</td><td>Red</td><td>Stop and switch off. Check the oil level.</td></tr><tr><td>Battery</td><td>Red</td><td>Drive straight to us; the alternator may be failing.</td></tr><tr><td>Engine</td><td>Amber</td><td>Book a diagnostic scan this week.</td></tr><tr><td>Tyre pressure</td><td>Amber</td><td>Check and inflate all four tyres.</td></tr></tbody></table></figure><!-- /wp:table -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Flashing engine light</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A flashing engine light usually means a misfire, which can damage the catalytic converter quickly. Reduce speed and get it checked the same day.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Our diagnostic scan costs $59 and comes with a written report in plain English, deducted from the repair if you go ahead.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "diagnostics"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "dashboard"
   ],
   "featured_media": "gears",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false
  },
  {
   "slug": "ceramic-coating-worth-it",
   "title": "Is ceramic coating worth the money?",
   "date": "2026-09-29T15:00:00",
   "excerpt": "Mila explains what a coating can and cannot do, and who should save their money.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Ceramic coatings have become the most asked-about service in our detailing bay. They are excellent, but they are not magic, and they are not for everyone.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:columns --><div class=\"wp-block-columns\"><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What it does</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Makes washing much quicker</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Beads water and resists grime</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Adds gloss and protects from bird lime</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --></div><!-- /wp:column --><!-- wp:column --><div class=\"wp-block-column\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What it does not</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Stop stone chips or deep scratches</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Replace regular washing</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Fix swirls: those must be polished first</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --></div><!-- /wp:column --></div><!-- /wp:columns -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you keep your car for years and wash it yourself, a coating pays for itself in time saved. If you lease for two years and use automatic car washes, a good sealant is the smarter buy.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:image {\"id\":{{media_id:detailing}},\"sizeSlug\":\"large\",\"linkDestination\":\"none\"} --><figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img src=\"{{media:detailing}}\" alt=\"Water beading on red paint\" class=\"wp-image-{{media_id:detailing}}\"/><figcaption class=\"wp-element-caption\">Water behaviour on a freshly coated bonnet.</figcaption></figure><!-- /wp:image -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Every coating we apply starts with a full decontamination and machine polish, because a coating locks in whatever is underneath it, good or bad.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "detailing"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "paint",
    "ceramic"
   ],
   "featured_media": "ceramic",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false,
   "comments": [
    {
     "author": "Priya K.",
     "email": "priya.k.@example.com",
     "content": "Went for the coating after reading this. Washing takes half the time now.",
     "date": "2026-10-03T10:20:00"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "slug": "when-to-change-brake-pads",
   "title": "When should you change your brake pads?",
   "date": "2026-09-15T09:30:00",
   "excerpt": "Squealing, grinding, a longer pedal: how to tell when your brakes need attention.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brake pads wear gradually, so the change is easy to miss. Most pads last between 25,000 and 60,000 miles depending on how and where you drive.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Listen and feel</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A high squeal when braking: the wear indicator is touching the disc.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A grinding noise: metal on metal, stop driving.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>The car pulls to one side under braking.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>The pedal feels softer or travels further.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Front pads usually wear twice as fast as rear ones. We measure all four at every service and photograph them so you can see for yourself.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Brake fluid matters too. It absorbs water over time, which lowers its boiling point, so we test it at every service and change it every two years.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "maintenance"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "brakes"
   ],
   "featured_media": "hero-coupe",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false
  },
  {
   "slug": "oil-change-intervals",
   "title": "Oil change intervals, without the myths",
   "date": "2026-08-30T10:00:00",
   "excerpt": "Every 3,000 miles? Every two years? What actually matters for your engine oil.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Modern synthetic oils last far longer than the oils of twenty years ago, so the old rule of changing every 3,000 miles is mostly a myth. But the opposite extreme is risky too.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What we recommend</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Follow the manufacturer's interval as a maximum.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Change it sooner if you mostly do short trips or tow.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Never go more than two years, whatever the mileage.</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Short journeys are the real enemy: the engine never warms up, and moisture and fuel collect in the oil. If that sounds like your week, an interim service halfway through the year is money well spent.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Whatever the interval, check the dipstick once a month on level ground with the engine cold. Topping up between services is normal on many engines.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "maintenance"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "oil"
   ],
   "featured_media": "oil",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false
  },
  {
   "slug": "new-alignment-rig",
   "title": "New in the workshop: a laser alignment rig",
   "date": "2026-08-18T13:00:00",
   "excerpt": "Our new four-wheel alignment rig measures to a hundredth of a degree. Here is why that matters.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This month we installed a new laser alignment rig on lift three. It measures all four wheels at once and prints a before-and-after report for every car.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why alignment matters</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A car that is slightly out of alignment wears its tyres unevenly, pulls to one side and uses more fuel. Hitting one pothole hard is often enough to knock it out.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>To celebrate, alignment checks are free until the end of September. A full adjustment is $79.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "workshop-news"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "tyres"
   ],
   "featured_media": "workshop",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false
  },
  {
   "slug": "detailing-at-home-two-bucket",
   "title": "Wash like a detailer: the two-bucket method",
   "date": "2026-08-04T17:00:00",
   "excerpt": "Most swirl marks come from washing, not driving. A simple routine that keeps your paint glossy.",
   "content": "<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Swirl marks, the fine circular scratches you see in the sun, are almost always caused by dirt dragged across the paint during washing. The fix is simple and costs almost nothing.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What you need</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:list --><ul class=\"wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Two buckets, one with a grit guard</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A pH-neutral car shampoo</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A soft wash mitt and a drying towel</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list -->\n\n<!-- wp:heading --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">The method</h2><!-- /wp:heading -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Rinse the car first. Dip the mitt in soapy water, wash one panel from top to bottom, then rinse the mitt in the plain-water bucket before going back to the soap. Dry with a clean towel, patting rather than rubbing.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->\n\n<!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Wash in the shade, never in direct sun, so the soap does not dry on the paint and leave spots. Start with the roof and finish with the dirtiest lower panels.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph -->",
   "categories": [
    "detailing"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "paint"
   ],
   "featured_media": "blue-coupe",
   "format": "standard",
   "sticky": false
  }
 ],
